        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying an OMA Treadmill</h2>
          <p>Most of these apply to any marketplace treadmill; they matter more where the brand has no US store to fall back on.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the horsepower in the title</h3>
              <p>OMA’s 7200EB is listed at 2.25, 2.5 and 3.0 HP in different places, and a Caroma buyer found 2.25 HP on a motor sold as 3.25. Horsepower in a marketplace title is the least reliable figure. Judge the machine on belt size, capacity, warranty and returns.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a short belt to run on</h3>
              <p>A 45-inch belt is a walking belt. Running on it means a shortened stride and a real risk of stepping off the back. If you want to run, buy a treadmill with at least a 55-inch deck.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Not recording the warranty on the day</h3>
              <p>OMA’s warranty varies by listing, and listings change. Screenshot the warranty, the specification and the seller’s name when you order, so you can show what you were promised if something fails.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Letting the returns window run out unopened</h3>
              <p>A treadmill in its box can sit for weeks. Assemble it and test every speed and incline setting inside the returns window, so a fault is a return rather than a warranty claim to a maker you may not be able to reach.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            OMA is a real treadmill brand sold to US buyers through Amazon, and the <strong class="text-white">OMA 7200EB</strong> is a folding walking-and-jogging machine whose
            agreed figures, 10 mph, three manual incline settings and 300 lb, are reasonable. Its belt is short,
            its motor rating varies by listing, and we found no US website or published warranty, so buy it
            only from a seller with clear terms, and screenshot them. The 7200EB was not listed on Amazon US
            when we checked; the OMA Amazon does list is a close sibling with an 8.1 mph top speed.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the price gap is small, the <strong class="text-white">Sunny Active 300 (SF-T724011)</strong> at about $600 or
            the <strong class="text-white">XTERRA TRX1200</strong> at $549.99 gives you the same format from a
            brand with a US store. For more, see our <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">folding treadmill roundup</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

