        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Are OMA treadmills a good brand?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              OMA makes conventional folding treadmills whose agreed figures, such as the 7200EB’s 10 mph top speed and 300 lb capacity, are reasonable for their price. The weaknesses are short belts, motor and belt figures that differ between the manual and the listings, and no US website or published US warranty that we could find. Buy one only through a seller with clear warranty and returns terms.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does OMA sell treadmills in the US?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, mainly through Amazon and marketplace sellers. OMA manuals are indexed against Amazon US product identifiers, the OMA-7200EA has a US FCC authorisation, and returned OMA treadmills have been sold at US liquidation auctions. We found no OMA website or support page for US buyers.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What are the OMA 7200EB’s specifications?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              OMA’s manual and listings agree on a 0.6 to 10 mph speed range, three manual incline levels, 36 preset programs, a folding frame and a 300 lb capacity. They disagree on the belt, 16.5 by 45.3 inches in the manual and 17 by 45.5 inches in listings, and on the motor, given as 2.25, 2.5 or 3.0 HP.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the OMA 7200EB good for running?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not really. Its belt, about 45 inches long by either published figure, is well short of the 55 to 60 inches usually recommended for running. Treat it as a walking and light-jogging treadmill, and if you want to run, look at a machine with a longer deck.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is Caroma the same as OMA?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              We found no source linking them. Caroma is best known as an Australian bathroom brand, and the Caroma-named treadmills we found were separate marketplace machines, including a used 3.0HP folding treadmill with a 15.75 by 47.24-inch running area and a 250 lb capacity.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty do OMA treadmills have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              We could not find a published OMA warranty for the US. Terms in individual listings varied, from one year on one incline model to three years on another, and one buyer mentioned a two-year term. Screenshot the warranty statement on the listing you buy from, and check the seller’s returns window.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
